   "source_id": "Likewise, $b$ is equivalent to four times the volume of the particle.",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "molecular-parameters",
   "prompt": "<p>Use $b = \\tfrac{2}{3}\\pi N_0 \\sigma^3$ to find the van der Waals $b$ of argon, with $\\sigma = 3.40$ Å (Section 16.6), in cm³/mol. Check that it is four times the volume of a mole of spheres of radius $\\sigma/2$. ($N_0 = 6.022 \\times 10^{23}$ mol⁻¹)</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "number",
    "value": 49.6,
    "unit": "cm³/mol",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "hint": "Work in centimeters: 3.40 Å is 3.40 × 10⁻⁸ cm.",
    "solution": "<p>$b = \\tfrac{2}{3}\\pi(6.022 \\times 10^{23}\\ \\text{mol}^{-1})(3.40 \\times 10^{-8}\\ \\text{cm})^3 = 49.6\\ \\text{cm}^3/\\text{mol}$. One sphere of radius $\\sigma/2 = 1.70$ Å has volume $\\tfrac{4}{3}\\pi(1.70 \\times 10^{-8}\\ \\text{cm})^3 = 2.06 \\times 10^{-23}\\ \\text{cm}^3$, a mole of them $12.4\\ \\text{cm}^3$, and four times that is $49.6\\ \\text{cm}^3/\\text{mol}$. It is also the hard-sphere $B_2$ of Section 16.5.</p>"
   }
  }
